##### **1.4 Natural Coordinates: Tangential and Normal Acceleration**

Curvilinear motion in two or three dimensions can be described intrinsically by tracking a particle along its trajectory using **natural coordinates** (path coordinates). Instead of fixed Cartesian axes, the reference frame is attached to the particle and oriented along the path. 

The trajectory is parameterized by the arc length $l(t)$ measured from an arbitrary reference point along the curve. The **speed** $v(t)$ is the time derivative of the traversed arc length:
$$v = \frac{dl}{dt}$$

At any point along the path, the direction of travel is specified by the **unit tangent vector** $\boldsymbol{\tau}(t)$, which has unit length $|\boldsymbol{\tau}| = 1$ and points along the instantaneous tangent to the trajectory. The instantaneous velocity vector $\boldsymbol{V}(t)$ is directed along this tangent:
$$\boldsymbol{V}(t) = v(t)\boldsymbol{\tau}(t)$$

Differentiating the velocity vector with respect to time yields the full acceleration vector $\boldsymbol{a}(t)$ via the product rule:
$$\boldsymbol{a} = \frac{d\boldsymbol{V}}{dt} = \frac{d}{dt}\bigl(v(t)\boldsymbol{\tau}(t)\bigr) = \frac{dv}{dt}\boldsymbol{\tau} + v\frac{d\boldsymbol{\tau}}{dt}$$

This separates the total acceleration into two orthogonal components:
$$\boldsymbol{a} = \boldsymbol{a}_\tau + \boldsymbol{a}_n$$
where $\boldsymbol{a}_\tau$ is the **tangential acceleration** and $\boldsymbol{a}_n$ is the **normal acceleration**.

